    | :sl:`repeatedly create an event on the event queue`
-   | :sg:`set_timer(eventid, milliseconds) -> None`
-   | :sg:`set_timer(eventid, milliseconds, once) -> None`
+   | :sg:`set_timer(event, milliseconds) -> None`
+   | :sg:`set_timer(event, milliseconds, once) -> None`
+   | :sg:`set_timer(event, milliseconds, once, repeat) -> None`
 
    Set an event type to appear on the event queue every given number of
    milliseconds. The first event will not appear until the amount of time has
    passed.
 
+   The event parameter can be an :class:`pygame.event.Event` object or an
+   integer representing the event type. Using an Event object allows you to
+   create timer events with custom attributes, enabling differentiation between
+   timers that share the same event type.
+
    Every event type can have a separate timer attached to it. It is best to use
    the value between ``pygame.USEREVENT`` and ``pygame.NUMEVENTS``.
 
@@ -66,8 +72,19 @@ resolution, in milliseconds, is given in the ``TIMER_RESOLUTION`` constant.
 
    If the once argument is True, then only send the timer once.
 
+   If the repeat argument is set to a positive integer, the timer will send
+   that many events before stopping. If repeat is 0 (the default), the timer
+   will continue to send events indefinitely.
+
+   The repeat argument takes precedence over the once argument if both are
+   specified.
+
    .. versionadded:: 2.0.0.dev3 once argument added.
 
+   .. versionadded:: 2.0.0.dev8 event parameter accepts Event objects.
+
+   .. versionadded:: 2.0.0.dev8 repeat argument added.
+
